@* One switchable option in the setup form's fine-tune panel: what it is, what it costs, a hint
   explaining it, and the switch. The two — an extra board, thematic maps — were written out in
   full twice, some fifty lines that differed in nothing but their words and whether the option
   can be refused.

   The row is the click target, not the switch, so it is a role="button" rather than a real one:
   the hint inside it is a button of its own and a button may not contain a button. That also
   means the keyboard has to be wired by hand — Space and Enter, as a native button would, and
   the focus ring every other clickable thing in the app wears (a div gets none for free).

   The row's chrome is .option-row, shared with the Settings page's owned-expansion rows — the
   same on/off row with a checkbox where this one has a switch. The pointer comes from the base
   layer's rule for [role="button"]; .is-disabled takes it away again.

   The hint is anchored centre rather than to its own marker: the sidebar this panel lives in is
   barely wider than the popover, and anchoring runs it off the card. *@

<div role="button" tabindex="0" aria-pressed="@Aria.Flag(On)" aria-disabled="@(Disabled ? "true" : null)"
     @onclick="Toggle" @onkeydown="OnKeyDown"
     class="option-row focus-ring select-none @(On ? "is-on" : null) @(Disabled ? "is-disabled" : "hover:border-ink-300")">
    <div class="flex min-w-0 items-start gap-1.5">
        <div>
            <div class="row-title">@Title</div>
            <div class="row-sub">@Sub</div>
        </div>
        @* The hint is a control of its own inside a control — its clicks and keys are its own. *@
        <span class="mt-0.5" @onclick:stopPropagation @onkeydown:stopPropagation>
            <InfoHint Position="center">@Hint</InfoHint>
        </span>
    </div>
    <ToggleSwitch On="On"/>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>The line under the title — what it costs, or why it cannot be had.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RenderFragment Sub { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>What the option actually does, in the popover behind the marker.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RenderFragment Hint { get; set; } = default!;

    [Parameter] public bool On { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The option isn't available on this table — shown, faded, and inert, so it does
    /// not come and go as the setup changes. <see cref="Sub"/> says why.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnToggle { get; set; }

    private Task Toggle() => Disabled ? Task.CompletedTask : OnToggle.InvokeAsync();

    /// Space and Enter, as the native button this cannot be would do.
    private Task OnKeyDown(KeyboardEventArgs e) =>
        e.Key is " " or "Enter" ? Toggle() : Task.CompletedTask;
}
